{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>By the blessing of the upright a city is exalted, and by the mouth of the wicked it is overthrown (Proverbs 11:11).</b>" "<b>King Solomon,</b> of blessed memory, mentioned in his book the entirety of all desirable and disgraceful traits, and among his words the righteous is set opposite the wicked, and the upright opposite the treacherous, for thus he aimed the traits precisely, one opposite the other — measure for measure, a thing and its opposite. And a full verse he mentioned in this regard is what is written (Proverbs 21:18): ״כופר לצדיק רשע ותחת ישרים בוגד״ — a ransom for the righteous, the wicked; and in place of the upright, the treacherous. He set the righteous opposite the wicked and the upright opposite the treacherous, and likewise he mentioned further (ibid. 11): ״צדקת ישרים תצילם וסלף בוגדים ישדם״ — the righteousness of the upright delivers them, but the crookedness of the treacherous destroys them. And so is his way everywhere therein, to set the order of the traits precisely one opposite the other, to mention the righteous opposite the wicked and the upright opposite the treacherous, for it is its complete opposite." "<b>And know</b> that the upright one is he who recognizes uprightness and loves it and chooses it, and he will endure any hard thing that uprightness requires of him, and he is the one who submits to truth; and the treacherous one is the opposite of all this — he does not recognize uprightness, and even if he recognizes it he will not acknowledge it, but in all his actions he does its opposite. And despite this, we find in some verses of the book of Proverbs of Solomon that he places in a single verse the trait of uprightness and the trait of the wicked, one opposite the other. And this verse says: ״בברכת ישרים תרום קרת״ — by the blessing of the upright a city is exalted — the upright man does good for the children of men, and the city in which he dwells within is elevated and exalted on his account; and it is not enough that he does them good with the words of his mouth, but also with his blessing, that is, with all that HaShem, may He be blessed, has bestowed upon him. And behold, the wicked one is the opposite of him — this is what is said: ״ובפי רשעים תהרס״ — and by the mouth of the wicked it is overthrown — for the wicked one, it is not enough that he does not do good to any person from what attaches to him, but rather the city in which he dwells is overthrown and destroyed by the words of his mouth and by the pride of his heart. And we learn that the destruction of the city is by the words of his mouth, whereas the righteous one is the glory of the city even with his money. And this is what it says: ״בברכת ישרים תרום קרת״. And the principle of the matter is that the righteous one is the cause of the exaltation of the city through his good deeds, and his words produce fruit for them. And this is what is written (Ecclesiastes 11:3): ״ואם יפול עץ בדרום ואם בצפון מקום שיפול העץ שם יהוא״ — and if a tree falls to the south or if to the north, in the place where the tree falls, there it shall be — this is a righteous man; that is, the tree, which is the righteous man, in the place of his dwelling — there are his fruits. ״ויצא יעקב״ etc. And when the righteous one departs from it, a great man departs from the land of Israel, and behold, his departure from the city makes an impression; and thus ״ויצא יעקב״. They said in the Midrash: (it need not have said other than) ״וילך חרנה״ — and he went to Haran — so what does ״ויצא״ teach? But to teach you that as long as the righteous one is in the city, he is its glory (הודה), he is its splendor (זיוה), he is its beauty (הדרה); when he departed from there, its glory departed, its splendor departed, its beauty departed. <small><b>[Says the printer:</b> this passage suffered from omissions, additions, and confusion of matter in the first transcription, and I, the undersigned, toiled and found to set the crooked straight, and whoever wishes may examine the old editions and see all this correction, <b>Yitzhak ben Yaakov, of blessed memory.]</b></small>" "<b>And Jacob went out from Beersheba.</b> Beersheba is in the land of Israel, for thus it is written above concerning Isaac: ״ויעל משם באר שבע״ — and he went up from there to Beersheba. And he called it Sheva (seven), therefore the name of the city is Beersheba, and he did not go outside the land." "<b>And behold,</b> the verses did not mention that Jacob went to Beersheba, but from the fact that it mentions ״ויצא״ (and he went out), you learn that he went there to take permission from HaShem, blessed be He, whether to go to Haran as his father and mother had commanded him, and for the honor of his father he went up there to take permission, and there the Holy One, blessed be He, promised him: ״אל תירא״ — do not fear. And thus did the Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpret: Jacob said: just as my father went to Beersheba, so too I am going to Beersheba; if He gives me permission, I am going out, and if not, I am not going out." "<b>And know</b> that had he not wished to take permission from the Holy One, blessed be He, it would have been fitting for the verse to say, 'And Jacob went out from Hebron,' for there Abraham and Isaac lived, and there he received the blessings. And the order of the matter is: 'And Jacob went out, and he encountered the place, and he lodged there.' The verse tells you that when he went out he encountered the place — that is, Mount Moriah. And after he lodged and lay down in that place, the Holy One, blessed be He, gave him permission to leave, in the manner of what He said: ״והנה אנכי עמך והשיבתיך אל האדמה הזאת״ — this is Be'er Sheva, which is in the land of Israel. 'And Jacob lifted up his feet and went toward the land of the children of the east.' For after he lay down in Be'er Sheva, Jacob rose early in the morning and lifted his feet toward the land of the children of the east. And behold, the entire passage comes after he encountered Mount Moriah and after he lodged in Be'er Sheva, and the entire matter at length after he descended. And what he included here, ״וילך חרנה״, is a specific detail; ״וישא יעקב רגליו וילך״ — to tell that he walked after the vision of the dream." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> ״ויצא יעקב מבאר שבע וילך חרנה״ — he went on the first day from Be'er Sheva to Haran, though there is a three-day journey there. It is alluded to by the language of the verse, which indicates the immediacy of the matter, and this is: מבאר שבע, וילך חרנה — and likewise the language at the end of the parashah: immediately as he lifted his feet. And likewise the language is written concerning Eliezer: ״ויקם וילך״. Three had the way shortened for them: Eliezer, servant of Abraham, and Jacob, and Abishai. Eliezer, as it is written: ״ואבא היום אל העין״ — 'I came today to the spring': today I set out and today I arrived." "<b>And in another place</b> they interpreted: ״ויפגע במקום״ — for when he reached Haran, he said: 'Is it possible that I passed by the place where my forefathers prayed and I did not pray there?' He set his mind to return, and the earth contracted for him immediately — ״ויפגע במקום״, that is, Mount Moriah. <br><b>And the explanation of</b> the Midrash is that when Jacob longed for Mount Moriah to pray there, immediately, in a sudden flash, the way was shortened for him and he did not need to return and exert himself at all. And according to this matter we learn of two contractions: the first, on his going from Be'er Sheva to Haran, where he went of his own will; and the second, so that he would not need to exert himself to return." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> Twelve stones he took from the stones of the altar upon which Isaac was bound — an allusion that twelve tribes were destined to arise from him, and they all became one stone, as it is said: ״ויקח את האבן אשר שם״ — an allusion that they were destined to be one nation, as it is said (I Chronicles 17:21): ״ומי כעמך ישראל גוי אחד בארץ״."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:16:12.481801763Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}